.TH SASH-EXPAND 1 "2026-11-12" "safebash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sash-expand \- expand a restricted KEY=value configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash-expand
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash-expand
reads a UTF-8 configuration file, expands a small set of variable
operations and writes the resulting assignments to standard output.
It does not execute or source shell code, export variables, or change
the invoking process's environment.
Its syntax is not the safebash language or a complete Bash assignment syntax.
.PP
Exactly one filename is required.
There are no options, stdin mode, or special
.B \-
filename.
In particular,
.B \-\-help
is treated as a filename, not a help request.
.SH INPUT FORMAT
Each nonblank, non-comment physical line contains
.BR KEY=value .
Leading and trailing whitespace on the whole line is removed.
Lines beginning with
.B #
after that trimming are comments.
Inline
.B #
characters are data.
.PP
The first equals sign separates the key and value.
Keys must match
.BR [A\-Za\-z_][A\-Za\-z0\-9_]* ;
spaces before the equals sign are not allowed.
Spaces immediately after it belong to the value.
A matching outer pair of single or double quotes is removed.
Both quote forms still permit expansion; single quotes do not have their
Bash literal meaning here.
.PP
The variable table starts with the inherited environment.
Each expanded assignment updates that table before the next line is read,
so later assignments can refer to earlier ones.
Repeated keys are allowed and each assignment is emitted.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.TP
.B $VAR, ${VAR}
Substitute the variable's current value, or an empty string if absent.
.TP
.B ${VAR:\-WORD}
Use WORD when VAR is absent or empty; otherwise use its value.
.TP
.B ${VAR:+WORD}
Use WORD when VAR is present and nonempty; otherwise use an empty string.
.TP
.B ${VAR%PAT}, ${VAR%%PAT}
Remove the shortest or longest matching suffix, respectively.
.TP
.B ${VAR#PAT}, ${VAR##PAT}
Remove the shortest or longest matching prefix, respectively.
.PP
Default and alternate text, and stripping patterns, are not recursively
expanded.
Stripping patterns recognize
.B *
and
.BR ? ,
not bracket classes or filesystem globbing.
A pattern that does not match leaves the value unchanged.
.PP
A backslash removes itself and quotes the next byte; it does not turn
.B \en
into a newline.
Command substitution, arithmetic substitution and backticks are not
evaluated and their expansion forms are rejected, as is indirect
parameter expansion.
.SH OUTPUT
Each processed assignment is written as
.B KEY=value
followed by a newline.
Only file assignments are emitted, not the whole inherited environment.
Values are not shell-quoted or escaped.
Do not source or evaluate this output as shell code.
.PP
Earlier assignments may have been written before a later input error.
The output is not transactional.
Embedded newlines from variable values are not escaped, so values are
not guaranteed to occupy a single output line.
.PP
The output is newline-delimited text, not the NUL-delimited field format
used by
.BR sash-getopt (1).
In safebash,
.B readline
reads a line into a scalar;
.B read
reads NUL-delimited array elements, not KEY=value map entries.
Constructing a map requires explicit parsing.
Pipe input remains tainted and must satisfy the application's normal
validation or explicit trust rules.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was processed.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong argument count, an unreadable/non-UTF-8 file, or a reported
configuration/expansion error.
.PP
The implementation defects below can instead cause a Rust panic and
exit 101.
.SH EXAMPLE
Given
.BR settings.conf :
.PP
.EX
ROOT=/srv/demo
BIN=${ROOT}/bin
LABEL='two words'
.EE
.PP
Run:
.PP
.EX
sash-expand settings.conf
.EE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.EX
ROOT=/srv/demo
BIN=/srv/demo/bin
LABEL=two words
.EE
.SH BUGS
Non-ASCII literal value text is currently corrupted by byte-wise
character conversion.
Prefix/suffix stripping of non-ASCII values can panic at an invalid
UTF-8 boundary.
These are implementation bugs, not a deliberate ASCII-only input contract.
.PP
A value consisting of a lone single or double quote currently panics
instead of being handled as input.
.PP
Some stdout write failures, including a broken pipe or full device,
also cause a Rust panic.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sash (1),
.BR sash-getopt (1),
.BR sash-convert (1)
